+# Building and checking Drum
+
+Use the selected full Xcode installation with Swift 6 support and its matching Metal Toolchain. Check `xcodebuild -version` and `xcrun --no-cache --find metal`. If Metal is unavailable, install the matching component through Xcode or `xcodebuild -downloadComponent MetalToolchain`. The check script diagnoses prerequisites; it does not download components or change the selected Xcode.
+
+The committed `Package.resolved` currently locks SwiftTerm 1.20.0 at `5d14406844143538cd8f8851d2d8a67c1fe443e5`. Its build plugin generates version metadata. Review the resolved plugin and generator before trusting them in Xcode. Plugin validation remains on by default. For an authorized headless run after that review, append `--allow-reviewed-plugin` to the commands below. This passes Xcode's `-skipPackagePluginValidation` for that invocation, which covers all package plugins, not just SwiftTerm. Revisit the review when the lockfile changes; do not change global trust settings to make a check pass. FlowDeck's existing invocation override does not affect a direct shell command.
+
+## Ordinary checks
+
+Run from the repository (or invoke the script by absolute path):
+
+```sh
+scripts/check-project.sh build Debug
+scripts/check-project.sh test Debug
+scripts/check-project.sh test Release
+# A distributable configuration build, when needed:
+scripts/check-project.sh build Release
+```
+
+The helper chooses the native macOS architecture, prevents automatic dependency version updates, and adds `ENABLE_TESTABILITY=YES` for Release tests. It excludes the opt-in performance suite from ordinary regression runs. Logs go to a fresh `build/checks/` directory; `--output-dir DIR` selects another location. Failed builds/tests retain their original exit code, invalid arguments/prerequisites return 2, and a successful Xcode invocation that emits any `warning:` returns 3. The full log is retained. Compiler warnings remain errors; tooling warnings are also reported and fail the zero-warning gate. Do not describe a run as warning-free when a tooling warning occurred.
+
+The [performance benchmark](performance-benchmark.md) is separate. Its display-link and capture endpoints do not establish screen presentation or the §8 frame-rate gate.
+
+## Project generation and troubleshooting
+
+Edit `project.yml` for project configuration, then run `xcodegen generate` and inspect the generated diff. Do not hand-edit `Drum.xcodeproj`. Run the appropriate ordinary checks after generation. When inspecting dependency APIs, use the checkout revision recorded in the lockfile; public methods are not necessarily open for subclass overrides.
+
+A resource-fork/Finder-attribute signing failure is not a reason to change source or signing policy. If the log identifies that problem, remove the offending attributes from the derived build product only and rebuild. Do not apply a blanket cleanup to the repository.
+
+## Launch and visual checks
+
+A completed build does not launch the app. When the task explicitly requests build and launch, wait for the build to finish, open the built app, and inspect its window; report launch separately from build success. Use the product path in the build output rather than assuming a DerivedData identifier. If an older process is still running, verify which product it belongs to before claiming the new build is active.
+
+For visual development checks that should preserve an existing shell session, use a separate preview app with a distinct bundle identifier, as documented in the [performance pass](performance.md). Keep the existing terminal process and user preferences intact. An explicit request to launch the new app should be carried through without treating this preview practice as a new approval requirement.
+
+The SwiftPM snapshot harness in `DrumBundle.swift` is historical diagnostic support. The Xcode project and ordinary checks above are the maintained build path.
